 for all loss functions of  course this means that our derived class  see all H has to reason Beyond H itself  okay to include the set of all functions  that we may compose with h and with  hypotheses from H but still it's an  explicit description of a class of  functions for which calibration and  multi-accuracy suffice to give Omni  prediction  restricting ourselves a bit further we  can look at the set of all Lipschitz  losses still known convex but we get an  even simpler class of functions that  requires us to reason about threshold  functions over our hypotheses in a sense  we can view these results in two ways on  the one hand it shows a feasibility  result for Lipschitz Omni predictors we  only have to learn uh you know a  threshold multi-cal multi-accurate  predictor on the other hand it shows a  barrier to achieving this multi-accuracy  over thresholds of our hypothesis class  as it implies a very strong Omni  predictor guarantee  we can also go in the other direction  considering a restricted class of convex  loss functions that arise from  generalized linear models  for this class of losses we show that no  modification to the hypothesis class is  necessary  in particular for any hypothesis class H  if P tilde is calibrated and H  multi-accurate then P tilde is actually  an omni predictor for the glm losses  over h  this result demonstrates the surprising  power of these weak forms of  multi-calibration demonstrating a strong  guarantee from only multi-accuracy with  overall calibration  so this concludes the overview of our  main conceptual results we have a number  of more technical results in the paper  along with many details that I've  glossed over but I hope you've come away  with an appreciation for the powerful  guarantee of omni prediction and the  versatility of outcome in  distinguishability as a principled  paradigm for learning  to conclude I want to leave you with a  more General perspective on oi we saw  how oi provided a clean recipe for 